 box one  so the cat's gonna have to move one box  and on the final day there's only one  possibility it has to be in box two  so this is the only possible place the  cat could be so we check that box and  we're going to win for sure  so now let's consider a different case  where the total number of boxes is an  even number  let's consider that there are four boxes  and the cat could be in any of the four  boxes  we'll split up the search into two  different cases let's suppose the cat  starts in an even numbered box that  would be box two or box four  we might as well search one of these  boxes so we'll get started by searching  box two  if the cat is there we win  otherwise we would deduce the cat must  have been in box four  the cat is going to move one box and  that means it's gonna have to move to  box three  therefore on the next day we can search  box three and we're going to win  so we're going to catch the cat using  the strategy if the cat started in an  even numbered box  but what if the cat started in an odd  numbered box  this case is going to be slightly  different the reason is that the cat is  still going to be in an odd numbered box  on the third day  let's see why suppose on the first day  the cat started in an odd numbered box  overnight it moves one box  and then it's going to move one more box  so on the third day the cat is still in  an odd numbered box so we're going to  have to catch a cat that is in an odd  numbered box  to do that we might as well search one  of the boxes  so in this case we're going to search  box three  we're going to do our search in reverse  we did two three now we're gonna do  three two so we start up by searching  box three if the cat is there we win  otherwise we can deduce the cat must  have been in box one  so overnight the cat is gonna move one  box it's gonna have to move to box two  so we're then going to search box two  and we're going to catch the cat for  sure  so notice that this search was going to  be 2 3 